.TH OTPME-INVENTORY 1 "2026-10-24" "OTPme 0.3.0a283" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-inventory \- show the system, the packages and the hardware of hosts, and what their systems offer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-inventory
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-inventory
shows which system a host runs, which kernel and which OTPme version it is running, whether it has to be rebooted, which packages are installed on it, and what it is made of.
You can also search for hosts that have a package, run a system version, a kernel or an OTPme version, wait for a reboot, or have certain hardware.
.PP
Use it to find the hosts a job has to run on, for example all hosts with an old version of a package.
See
.BR otpme\-deploy (1).
Use it to find a machine by its serial number, or to see how much memory and disk a host has.
Use it to find the hosts that still have to be rebooted, see \fBWHEN A HOST HAS TO BE REBOOTED\fR.
Use it to find the hosts that still run an old OTPme, see \fBOTPME VERSION\fR.
Use it to see which packages a system offers and which hosts have a package that can be updated, see \fBREFERENCE HOSTS\fR.
.PP
The \fBconfd\fR daemon on each host (and node) reports this data to the nodes of its site.
It checks the host every 60 seconds and only sends what changed.
It does not take stock while a job is running on the host.
A package manager at work is no good moment for it, the next round does it.
.PP
The OS, the OS version, the kernel, the reboot, the OTPme version, the packages and the hardware are stored in a separate inventory object for each host, not on the host object.
All of it is what the host reported about itself, and an inventory has to stand on its own.
Host objects are synced to all hosts, and the package lists of all hosts should not be, so the inventory stays on the nodes of the host's site.
.PP
Changing the inventory does not change the last modified time of the host.
.PP
To see the inventory of a host your token has to be named in the
.B allow_inventory
config parameter of that host.
Hosts you may not see are left out of search results.
See \fBPERMISSIONS\fR below.
.PP
Shown are the hosts whose inventory this site keeps: its own, and those of other sites that report here, see \fBA CENTRAL INVENTORY\fR.
.PP
The hardware is read on any Linux host, and the system, the kernel and the OTPme version on any host at all.
Package lists need a package manager the host knows, which today means Debian and Ubuntu, and so does the answer whether the host has to be rebooted.
A host without one still reports its system, its kernel, its OTPme version and its hardware.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B show \fIhost\fR
Show the OS, the OS version, the architecture, the running kernel, whether the host has to be rebooted, the OTPme version, the number of packages, the vendor, the model, the serial number and the time of the last update.
.TP
.B packages \fR[\fB\-\-filter\fR \fIpattern\fR] \fIhost\fR
List the packages of a host, one per line with name and version.
.TP
.B hardware \fIhost\fR
Show what a host is made of: CPU, memory, disks, serial number and so on.
Sizes are shown in human form, for example \fB9.7G\fR.
Says so if the host has not reported its hardware yet.
.TP
.B search \fR[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost\fR] [\fB\-\-os\-version\fR \fIversion\fR] [\fB\-\-kernel\fR \fIkernel\fR] [\fB\-\-otpme\-version\fR \fIversion\fR] [\fB\-\-reboot\-required\fR] [\fB\-\-hardware\fR \fIexpression\fR] [\fB\-\-upgradable\fR] [\fIpackage\fR]
Find hosts by package, by OS version, by kernel, by OTPme version, by a waiting reboot, by hardware, or by packages that can be updated.
\fIpackage\fR is a package name, or a name and a version in the form \fIname\fB=\fIversion\fR.
Both can contain wildcards (\fB*\fR).
Every option you give has to match, so they narrow the result down.
You have to give at least one of them.
.IP
Each line says a host and what made it match.
With a \fIpackage\fR that is the package and its version, one line per package.
With \fB\-\-hardware\fR it is the value that matched.
With \fB\-\-reboot\-required\fR it is the host name alone, because the answer is the same for every host that is listed.
A host has several disks, and then only the disks that match are shown, with their name.
So a search for \fB'disk_size>=100G'\fR shows \fBsdb=150.0G\fR and not all disks of the host.
Behind the size comes what says which disk it is, the vendor, the model and the serial, where the host knows them: \fBsdb=150.0G(SEAGATE ST1000NM0055 ZA12345)\fR.
With \fB\-\-upgradable\fR it is the package, the version installed and the version the system offers: \fBopenssl=3.0.15\-1~deb12u1 \-> 3.0.17\-1~deb12u1\fR.
.TP
.B systems
List the systems the hosts run, one line per OS version and architecture: how many hosts run it, the reference host that reports what it offers, how many packages that is, and when it last reported.
A system nobody reports shows \fB\-\fR there.
Only systems with a host you may see are listed, and only those hosts are counted.
See \fBREFERENCE HOSTS\fR.
.TP
.B available \fR[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost\fR] [\fB\-\-os\-version\fR \fIversion\fR] [\fB\-\-arch\fR \fIarch\fR] \fIpackage\fR
List the packages a system offers: what can be installed on a host that runs it.
\fIpackage\fR works as with \fBsearch\fR, a name or \fIname\fB=\fIversion\fR, with wildcards.
The version is the one apt would install.
.IP
With \fB\-\-host\fR it is the system of that host.
Each line is a package, and behind it the version installed on the host, if it is: \fBopenssl=3.0.17\-1~deb12u1 installed=3.0.15\-1~deb12u1\fR.
.IP
Without it, \fB\-\-os\-version\fR and \fB\-\-arch\fR say which systems, with wildcards, and each line starts with its system: \fBdebian\-12 amd64 openssl=3.0.17\-1~deb12u1\fR.
Without them it is every system you may see.
.IP
It needs a reference host for the system, see \fBREFERENCE HOSTS\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-filter \fIpattern\fR
Only list packages whose name matches the pattern, for example \fB'python3\-*'\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-host \fIhost\fR
For \fBsearch\fR: only this host.
Use it to search the packages of one host instead of listing all of them.
For \fBavailable\fR: the system of this host, see there.
.TP
.B \-\-os\-version \fIversion\fR
Only hosts with this OS version, for example \fBdebian\-12\fR, or for \fBavailable\fR only this system.
Wildcards are allowed, for example \fB'debian\-*'\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-arch \fIarch\fR
For \fBavailable\fR: only this architecture, for example \fBamd64\fR.
Wildcards are allowed.
.TP
.B \-\-upgradable
For \fBsearch\fR: only installed packages of which the system of the host offers a newer version.
Takes no value.
With a \fIpackage\fR only that package.
A host whose system no reference host reports is left out, and so is a package its system does not offer.
See \fBREFERENCE HOSTS\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-kernel \fIkernel\fR
Only hosts running this kernel, for example \fB6.1.0\-37\-amd64\fR.
Wildcards are allowed, for example \fB'6.1.0\-3*'\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-otpme\-version \fIversion\fR
Only hosts running this OTPme version, for example \fB0.3.0a283\fR.
Wildcards are allowed, for example \fB'0.3.0a28*'\fR.
See \fBOTPME VERSION\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-reboot\-required
Only hosts that have to be rebooted to run what is installed on them.
Takes no value.
Hosts that answered no, and hosts that cannot say, are left out.
See \fBWHEN A HOST HAS TO BE REBOOTED\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-hardware \fIexpression\fR
Only hosts whose hardware matches.
The expression is a field, an operator and a value.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fIfield\fB=\fIvalue\fR matches the value. Wildcards are allowed, for example \fB'model=PowerEdge*'\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIfield\fB>\fIvalue\fR, \fIfield\fB<\fIvalue\fR, \fIfield\fB>=\fIvalue\fR and \fIfield\fB<=\fIvalue\fR compare.
Only fields that are a number can be compared.
.PP
Put the expression in quotes.
\fB>\fR and \fB<\fR are redirections in the shell, and \fB*\fR is a file name pattern.
.RE
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Who may read the inventory of a host is set with the
.B allow_inventory
config parameter.
It holds tokens and roles.
A token may read the inventory when it is named, or when it is in a role that is named.
Roles inside roles count too.
.PP
You can set it on a host, on a unit, on a site.
The most specific one wins, like every config parameter.
So you can allow a role on one unit and nowhere else.
.PP
If it is not set, nobody may read the inventory.
The admin token and tokens in the admin role are the exception, they always may.
For a node only the realm admin may, not a site admin, the same as for an ACL on a node.
.PP
This is a config parameter and not an ACL on purpose.
An inventory is not one more attribute of a host.
It is the serial number, what is installed and what the machine is built from.
With an ACL a plain \fBview\fR on the host would carry all of it, and \fBview\fR is given out so somebody can read a status.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
otpme\-host config client1 allow_inventory 'role|example.org/berlin/it\-support'
.RE
.PP
What a system offers (\fBsystems\fR, \fBavailable\fR) belongs to no host, so no \fBallow_inventory\fR names it.
It follows the hosts: you may read what a system offers when you may read the inventory of at least one host that runs it.
.PP
For a host of another site that reports here, see \fBA CENTRAL INVENTORY\fR, there is a second parameter:
.TP
.B allow_foreign_inventory \fR(list)
Who may read the inventories of hosts that are not ours.
Set on the site that keeps them, it holds tokens and roles like \fBallow_inventory\fR and decides instead of the \fBallow_inventory\fR of the host.
.br
Unset means the host decides, with its own \fBallow_inventory\fR at the host, its unit and its site of origin.
.br
It does not touch the hosts of this site, those always go by \fBallow_inventory\fR.
.br
Object types: site
.SH A CENTRAL INVENTORY
By default a host reports to the site it belongs to, and that site answers for it.
A realm that wants one place to ask "which machines do we have" points its hosts somewhere else.
.PP
Two parameters, one at each end, because a host saying where it wants to report is not the same as a site agreeing to keep it.
.TP
.B inventory_site \fR(str)
Where a host reports its inventory.
Set it on a host, a unit or a site; the most specific one wins, like every config parameter.
Unset means the site the host belongs to.
.br
Object types: site, unit, host, node
.TP
.B inventory_trusts \fR(list)
The sites whose hosts this site keeps inventories for.
Unset means none, and then a host of another site is refused.
.br
Object types: site
.PP
Example, with every host of \fIberlin\fR reporting to \fImunich\fR:
.PP
.RS
.nf
otpme\-site config berlin inventory_site munich
otpme\-site config munich inventory_trusts berlin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both sites have to be in the same realm.
An inventory is kept per realm, and a host of another realm is refused whatever is set here.
.PP
Who may read them is the
.B allow_foreign_inventory
of this site, and where that is unset the
.B allow_inventory
of the host, which resolves at that host, its unit and its site of origin.
So either end can decide: set it here and this site says who reads, leave it and the site the data is about keeps saying.
See \fBPERMISSIONS\fR.
.PP
A host that reported to its own site before keeps that inventory there.
It is not moved and not deleted, and it stops being updated.
.SH OS, OS VERSION AND KERNEL
\fBos\fR is \fBLinux\fR, \fBWindows\fR or \fBMacOS\fR.
\fBos_version\fR is the ID and the version from \fI/etc/os\-release\fR, for example \fBdebian\-13\fR or \fBubuntu\-24.04\fR.
A system without a version number uses its code name, for example \fBdebian\-trixie\fR.
.PP
\fBarch\fR is the architecture the way the packages name it, for example \fBamd64\fR or \fBarm64\fR, from \fBdpkg \-\-print\-architecture\fR.
A host without dpkg does not report one.
.PP
\fBkernel\fR is the kernel the host is running, the way \fBuname \-r\fR says it, for example \fB6.1.0\-37\-amd64\fR.
.PP
Running, not installed.
A host that took a kernel update and was not rebooted has the new kernel installed and the old one running, and its package list cannot tell you that: the package is there either way.
So this is the one value an inventory cannot work out from the rest of itself.
.PP
The kernel packages of a host say which kernels are installed, and
.B show
says which one is running.
A host where the two do not match is waiting for a reboot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-host server1 'linux\-image\-6*'
server1 linux\-image\-6.1.0\-38\-amd64=6.1.146\-1
$ otpme\-inventory show server1
host: server1
os: Linux
os_version: debian\-12
arch: amd64
kernel: 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
\&...
.fi
.RE
.PP
You do not have to compare them yourself.
The host does it and says so, see \fBWHEN A HOST HAS TO BE REBOOTED\fR.
.SH WHEN A HOST HAS TO BE REBOOTED
.B reboot_required
is \fByes\fR when the host has to be rebooted to run what is installed on it, \fBno\fR when it does not, and \fB\-\fR when the host cannot say.
.PP
The host works this out itself, because what says it differs from system to system.
On Debian and Ubuntu two things are asked:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/var/run/reboot\-required\fR, the file a package writes when it needs a reboot.
Not only the kernel does that, libc and systemd ask for one too.
The file is written by \fBupdate\-notifier\-common\fR or by \fBneedrestart\fR.
Ubuntu has one of them, Debian usually has neither, and then the file never shows up however much the host needs a reboot.
.IP \(bu 2
The kernel, compared.
The newest kernel that is installed against the one that is running, and for a kernel that was updated in place \(em same \fBuname \-r\fR, new package version \(em the version of the package against the version the running kernel was built from.
This needs nothing installed, and the kernel is the reason for a reboot in nearly every case.
.RE
.PP
So a plain Debian host says \fByes\fR for a kernel and \fBno\fR for everything else.
Install
.B needrestart
there if a reboot that libc or systemd asks for should show up as well.
.PP
\fB\-\fR means the host said nothing.
Either there is no handler for its system, and then it reports its kernel and its hardware but no packages and no reboot, or its running kernel has no package to compare against \(em a kernel built by hand \(em and nothing on it writes the file.
.PP
A host that says \fByes\fR goes on saying it until it is rebooted.
Reboot it with
.BR otpme\-deploy (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
otpme\-deploy add \-\-host server1 \-\-expiry 1h host_mgmt reboot
.fi
.RE
.SH OTPME VERSION
.B otpme_version
is the OTPme version the host is running, for example \fB0.3.0a283\fR.
.PP
Running, not installed, the same as the kernel.
It is the version of the \fBconfd\fR daemon that reports it.
A host that got a new OTPme and was not restarted still runs the old one, and that is what you see here.
.PP
\fB\-\fR means the host has not reported a version yet.
An OTPme older than this field sends none.
.SH REFERENCE HOSTS
A reference host reports the packages its system offers: what can be installed on a host with the same OS version and architecture.
With that,
.B available
shows what can be installed, and
.B search \-\-upgradable
finds the hosts that have a package in an older version than their system offers.
.PP
Set them on the site that keeps the inventory of the hosts.
That is the site they belong to, or their \fBinventory_site\fR, see \fBA CENTRAL INVENTORY\fR.
The site that keeps the data decides, like with \fBinventory_trusts\fR:
.TP
.B reference_hosts \fR(list)
The hosts that report to this site the packages their system offers.
One per OS version and architecture is enough.
.br
Object types: site
.PP
The host does not read the parameter.
The site tells it with every answer about its inventory.
So a host learns that it is a reference host, or is no longer one, the next time it reports its inventory, at the latest after an hour.
.PP
Example, with one Debian 12 and one Debian 13 host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
otpme\-site config berlin reference_hosts refhost12,refhost13
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBconfd\fR daemon on a reference host reads its package lists and sends what changed, the same way every host sends its own packages.
It reads them when they changed or once an hour.
It does not run \fBapt update\fR itself.
That is the job of the \fBapt\-daily\fR timer of the host, so what is offered is as new as the last \fBapt update\fR there.
The version is the one apt would install, so pins count.
Packages for the architecture of the host and for \fBall\fR are reported, packages of a foreign architecture are not.
.PP
The packages go where the host sends its inventory, and the reference host has to be one that site takes the inventory of.
The site takes them for the OS version and architecture that the inventory of the host says, and for no other.
They are kept in one object per OS version and architecture, on the nodes of that site.
.PP
One reference host writes an object.
A second one with the same system waits.
It takes over when the first one has not reported for three days, is deleted, or is no longer in \fBreference_hosts\fR.
So a second reference host is a stand\-in, and two with different repositories do not take turns.
.PP
Who may read what a system offers follows the hosts: a token that may read the inventory of a host that runs the system may read what it offers, see \fBPERMISSIONS\fR.
.SH PACKAGE NAMES
Package names are the names dpkg uses.
A multi-arch package has its architecture in the name, for example \fBlibc6:amd64\fR.
Only fully installed packages are listed.
.PP
The packages a system offers are named without the architecture.
\fBsearch \-\-upgradable\fR and \fBavailable \-\-host\fR compare \fBlibc6:amd64\fR on a host with \fBlibc6\fR of its system.
.SH HARDWARE FIELDS
The host reads all of this from files, so nothing has to be installed for it.
A field that a host cannot read is left out.
On a virtual machine most of the DMI fields are empty.
.br
The one thing that is nicer with a package installed is the name of a controller: it comes from the PCI ID database (\fBpciutils\fR or \fBhwdata\fR), and without it a controller is its IDs and its driver.
.PP
Everything a host reports can be searched with \fB\-\-hardware\fR.
These are the fields:
.TP
.B vendor\fR, \fBmodel\fR, \fBserial\fR, \fBasset_tag\fR, \fBsku
What the machine says about itself, from \fI/sys/class/dmi/id\fR.
.B serial
is what Dell calls the Service Tag.
You can also write
.B service_tag
in a search, it means the same field.
.B asset_tag
is the tag your own IT sets, it is a different field.
.TP
.B board_vendor\fR, \fBboard_model\fR, \fBboard_serial
The same three for the mainboard.
Useful when a board series turns out to be faulty: the machine can carry the name of the maker who built it, the board carries the name of who made the board.
.TP
.B cpu_model\fR, \fBcpu_count
The CPU and how many logical CPUs there are, from \fI/proc/cpuinfo\fR.
.B cpu_count
can be compared.
.TP
.B memory
How much memory the host has, from \fI/proc/meminfo\fR.
Can be compared.
Write it as a size, for example \fB8G\fR.
.TP
.B disk\fR, \fBdisk_size\fR, \fBdisk_serial\fR, \fBdisk_vendor\fR, \fBdisk_model
One entry per disk, from \fI/sys/block\fR.
.B disk
is the name, for example \fBsda\fR.
.B disk_size
is the size and can be compared.
.B disk_serial
is the serial number of the disk, the one on the sticker.
.B disk_vendor
and
.B disk_model
are who made it and what it is called, for example \fBATA\fR and \fBOCZ\-VERTEX3 MI\fR.
Use them when a whole series of disks turns out to be faulty.
.br
A SATA disk says \fBATA\fR as its vendor, whatever is written on it: that is the name of the interface and not of the maker, and the maker is part of the model.
A SAS or SCSI disk says \fBSEAGATE\fR or \fBDELL\fR.
So search a SATA disk by its model.
.br
A host matches when any of its disks matches, and the answer names the disks that did.
.br
The serial is read from the disk itself, so not every disk has one: NVMe disks report it directly, SCSI and SATA disks through their serial number page.
.TP
.B nic
The network cards, from \fI/sys/class/net\fR.
Only what has hardware behind it, so the loopback, the bridges, the bonds, the VLAN interfaces and the tunnels are left out: those are not hardware and are named after whoever configured them.
.br
Matches the name the kernel gave a card (\fBeno1\fR), its MAC, its vendor, the name on the card or the name of the chip on it.
Search it by MAC when a DHCP lease or a switch port is all you have and the question is which host that is.
.TP
.B controller
The storage controllers, from \fI/sys/bus/pci/devices\fR: every PCI device of the mass storage class.
Matches the PCI address of a controller (\fB0000:18:00.0\fR), its vendor, the name on the card (\fBPERC H730P Mini\fR) or the name of the chip on it (\fBMegaRAID SAS\-3 3108\fR).
.br
The answer is the disks that hang on the controller, because that is what a controller is looked for.
.br
The names come from the PCI ID database of the host (\fI/usr/share/misc/pci.ids\fR or \fI/usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids\fR).
A host without one reports the IDs and the driver, and then only those can be searched.
.TP
.B virtual\fR, \fBvirt_type
Whether the host is a virtual machine, and on what.
.B virtual
is \fByes\fR or \fBno\fR.
.B virt_type
is \fBkvm\fR, \fBvmware\fR, \fBvirtualbox\fR, \fBxen\fR, \fBhyperv\fR and so on.
.TP
.B bios_vendor\fR, \fBbios_version\fR, \fBbios_date
The BIOS.
.B bios_date
is written the way the board writes it, usually \fBmm/dd/yyyy\fR, so search it with a wildcard.
.TP
.B chassis_type
What kind of case this is (\fBrack\-mount\-chassis\fR, \fBlaptop\fR, \fBdesktop\fR and so on).
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS One host
Show the system of a host, how many packages it has and which machine it is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory show client1
host: client1
os: Linux
os_version: debian\-13
arch: amd64
kernel: 6.12.43\-amd64
reboot_required: no
otpme_version: 0.3.0a283
packages: 686
vendor: Dell Inc.
model: PowerEdge R640
serial: ABC1234
last_update: 2026\-09\-24 20:13
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show what it is made of:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory hardware client1
bios_version: 2.19.1
chassis_type: rack\-mount\-chassis
cpu_count: 16
cpu_model: Intel(R) Xeon(R) Silver 4216
memory: 31.2G
model: PowerEdge R640
serial: ABC1234
vendor: Dell Inc.
virtual: no
disk: sda 223.6G DELL PERC H730P serial=6F4EE0803A2E4900 controller=0000:18:00.0
disk: sdb 1.8T DELL PERC H730P serial=6F4EE0803A2E4901 controller=0000:18:00.0
controller: 0000:18:00.0 Broadcom / LSI PERC H730P Mini MegaRAID SAS\-3 3108 vendor_id=1000 device_id=005d subsystem_id=1028:1f47 driver=megaraid_sas
nic: eno1 ec:f4:bb:11:22:33 Intel Corporation I350 Gigabit Network Connection vendor_id=8086 device_id=1521 driver=igb
.fi
.RE
.PP
List its packages, all of them or only some:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory packages client1
$ otpme\-inventory packages \-\-filter 'libreoffice*' client1
.fi
.RE
.SS Find hosts by package
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search openssl
Show all hosts that have the package openssl, with its version.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search 'openssl=3.0.*'
Show the hosts that have a 3.0 version of openssl.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-host client1 'python3\-*'
Show the python3 packages of client1.
Without \fB\-\-host\fR this asks every host.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-os\-version debian\-12 firefox\-esr
Show the Debian 12 hosts that have firefox\-esr.
.PP
One line per package, so a host with four matching packages is on four lines:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-host client1 'libc6*'
client1 libc6\-dbg:amd64=2.36\-9+deb12u14
client1 libc6\-dev:amd64=2.36\-9+deb12u14
client1 libc6\-i386=2.36\-9+deb12u14
client1 libc6:amd64=2.36\-9+deb12u14
.fi
.RE
.SS Find hosts by system
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-os\-version debian\-12
Show all hosts that run Debian 12.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-os\-version 'debian\-*'
Show all Debian hosts, whatever the version.
.SS Find hosts by kernel
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-kernel 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
Show all hosts running this kernel.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-kernel '6.1.0\-3*'
Show all hosts running a 6.1.0\-3 kernel.
.PP
The line says the kernel, so this is also how you see which kernels are running at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-kernel '*'
client1 6.12.43\-amd64
dev1 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
server1 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
server2 6.1.0\-38\-amd64
.fi
.RE
.PP
A host that has not reported since the kernel was added shows nothing here.
It is left out, the same as a host that never reported at all.
.SS Find hosts that have to be rebooted
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-reboot\-required
Show all hosts that have to be rebooted to run what is installed on them.
.PP
The host name alone, because every host listed is one that has to be rebooted:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-reboot\-required
dev1
server1
server2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Which kernel each of them runs is one question further:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-reboot\-required \-\-kernel '*'
dev1 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
server1 6.1.0\-37\-amd64
server2 6.1.0\-38\-amd64
.fi
.RE
.SS Find hosts by OTPme version
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-otpme\-version 0.3.0a283
Show all hosts running this OTPme version.
.PP
The line says the version, so this is how you see which hosts still run an old one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-otpme\-version '*'
client1 0.3.0a283
dev1 0.3.0a283
server1 0.3.0a281
.fi
.RE
.PP
A host that has not reported a version yet is left out.
.SS Find hosts by hardware
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware service_tag=ABC1234
Find the machine with this Dell service tag.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware 'model=PowerEdge*'
Show all Dell PowerEdge servers.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware virtual=no
Show all hosts that are not virtual machines.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware 'memory<8G'
Show all hosts with less than 8 GB of memory.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware 'cpu_count>=8'
Show all hosts with 8 CPUs or more.
.PP
The line says what matched, so a search for memory shows the memory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware 'memory>=4G'
client1 7.8G
dev1 9.7G
server1 31.2G
.fi
.RE
.PP
A host has several disks, so only the disks that match are shown:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware 'disk_size>=100G'
client1 sda=223.6G(DELL PERC H730P 6F4EE0803A2E4900)
dev1 sdc=100.0G(QEMU QEMU HARDDISK)
server1 sdb=150.0G(SEAGATE ST1000NM0055 ZA12345)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The other way round, to find the host a disk sits in when all you have is the disk:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware disk_serial=ZA12345
server1 sdb=150.0G(SEAGATE ST1000NM0055 ZA12345)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or every host that has a disk of a model, when a series turns out to be faulty:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware 'disk_model=ST1000NM*'
server1 sdb=150.0G(SEAGATE ST1000NM0055 ZA12345)
server2 sdb=150.0G(SEAGATE ST1000NM0055 ZA67890)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for a controller, and the answer is the disks that hang on it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware 'controller=PERC H730P*'
server1 PERC H730P Mini(sda=223.6G,sdb=1.8T)
server2 PERC H730P Mini(sda=223.6G)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Which host a MAC belongs to:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware nic=ec:f4:bb:11:22:33
server1 eno1=ec:f4:bb:11:22:33(Intel Corporation I350 Gigabit Network Connection)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search by disk name the same way.
\fBdisk=sda\fR shows the \fBsda\fR of every host with its size:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware disk=sda
client1 sda=223.6G(DELL PERC H730P 6F4EE0803A2E4900)
dev1 sda=48.0G(QEMU QEMU HARDDISK)
server1 sda=32.0G(QEMU QEMU HARDDISK)
.fi
.RE
.SS Narrow it down
Every option has to match, so you can put them together.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-hardware 'disk_size>1T' \-\-os\-version debian\-13
The Debian 13 hosts that have a disk larger than 1 TB.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-os\-version debian\-12 \-\-hardware virtual=no openssl
The physical Debian 12 machines that have openssl.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-os\-version debian\-12 \-\-kernel '6.1.0\-37*'
The Debian 12 hosts that still run this kernel.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-reboot\-required \-\-os\-version debian\-12
The Debian 12 hosts that have to be rebooted.
.SS What can be installed and updated
These need a reference host, see \fBREFERENCE HOSTS\fR.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory systems
debian\-12 amd64 hosts=14 reference_host=refhost12 packages=63644 last_report=2026\-10\-24 09:12
debian\-13 amd64 hosts=3 reference_host=refhost13 packages=65102 last_report=2026\-10\-24 09:15
ubuntu\-24.04 amd64 hosts=1 reference_host=\- packages=\- last_report=\-
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B otpme-inventory available \-\-os\-version debian\-13 'python3\-django*'
The Django packages Debian 13 offers.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory available \-\-host client1 openssl
The openssl that can be installed on client1, and the one installed there.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-upgradable openssl
The hosts with an openssl older than their system offers.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-upgradable \-\-host server1
Every package of server1 that can be updated.
.TP
.B otpme-inventory search \-\-upgradable \-\-os\-version debian\-12 'linux\-image\-*'
The Debian 12 hosts with a kernel package that can be updated.
